From 862b0e9db66392df77e8ca6b79e84003450d6083 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dr Jonathan Richard Robert Kimmitt <jonathan@kimmitt.uk>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 14:24:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] map: optional register retiming in timing mode
 (--map-only-retime <rounds>)

After the best timing-driven pass is chosen, each round moves logic across flip-flops from
the late side to the side with time in hand, and is kept when the analysis of the
re-emitted netlist has a better worst slack, or a better total with the worst slack within
a two-hundredth of the shortest period.

Forward: a LUT (up to six inputs) whose inputs all come straight from flip-flops with one
clock, enable and reset, and whose output is late while their D inputs have more than a
LUT's delay in hand, is rebuilt on the D side and one flip-flop (<register>_<bit>_fret<n>)
holds its value, at reset and power-up the LUT's value for its inputs'. A flip-flop left
without readers goes; the one a move makes is a candidate in later rounds, so a register
can go forward through several LUTs.

Backward: a register bit whose D input is late and whose output has the time is moved
behind the LUT that drives D (read by that D input alone): the LUT drives the bit's Q net
from flip-flops on each of its inputs (<register>_<bit>_bret<input>), with reset and
power-up values for which the LUT gives the bit's own. Not where the bit's enable is itself
late. The LUTs reading a moved bit read the moved LUT by name, so the fanout limit copies
it as it copies any LUT.

--map-only-retime-forward / -backward restrict the rounds to one direction;
--map-only-retime-keep keeps every round, for testing the moves themselves.
---
 src/V3EmitMap.cpp | 536 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 src/V3Options.cpp |   5 +
 src/V3Options.h   |   8 +
 3 files changed, 540 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

